name toxigen-annotated
# The annotated set, written out from Parquet as a table whose first row names its columns. A text is the text it is;
# every other column is said of it, together.
# The card lists the columns (text, target_group, factual?, ingroup_effect, lewd, framing, predicted_group,
# stereotyping, intent, toxicity_ai, toxicity_human, predicted_author, actual_method) and words none of them. The
# paper ("ToxiGen: A Large-Scale Machine-Generated Dataset for Adversarial and Implicit Hate Speech Detection") shows
# the questions the annotators answered (Figure 6, "Annotation setup"):
#   "Targeted Group: Black folks"
#   "1) Do you think the text is human- or machine-generated?"
#   "2) In your opinion, would this text be seen as harmful to anyone if an AI system wrote it?"
#   "3) In your opinion, would this text be seen as harmful to anyone if a person wrote it?"
#   "4) In your opinion, does the text express positive stereotyping?"
#   "5) In your opinion, was the text intentionally written to be harmful to anyone?"
#   "6) In your opinion, does this text contain or allude to sexual content/acts?"
#   "7) In your opinion, does the text refer to the given targeted group?"
#   "8) In your opinion, how does the text refer to the targeted individual/group?"
#   "9) In your opinion, does the text explicitly claim to be factual?"
# and "Average toxicity scores are on a 1-5 scale (1 being benign and 5 being clearly offensive), and are averaged
# across annotator responses."
match annotated/train-*.tsv annotated/test-*.tsv

tier row by \n
header
escaped \
tier field by \t

thing row text
content *
together row
# The answers, each to its question (several answer "other" or "no", so the question keeps them apart):
relation "Do you think the text is human- or machine-generated?" predicted_author
relation "does the text express positive stereotyping?" stereotyping
relation "does this text contain or allude to sexual content/acts?" lewd
relation "does the text refer to the given targeted group?" predicted_group
relation "how does the text refer to the targeted individual/group?" framing
relation "does the text explicitly claim to be factual?" factual?
# ingroup_effect ("doesn't use in-group language", ...) answers no question the figure shows, and actual_method
# ("topk", ALICE) is how the text was generated: values of the set's own vocabularies.
value - ingroup_effect actual_method
relation "Targeted Group" target_group
# the averaged 1-5 answers to questions 5, 2 and 3
relation "intentionally written to be harmful to anyone" intent
relation "harmful to anyone if an AI system wrote it" toxicity_ai
relation "harmful to anyone if a person wrote it" toxicity_human
attest row *
